 ### View Selection for Optimization
 Selects specific views for adaptation optimization to prevent overfitting to known training views and maintain novel-view performance [S4]. Steps: 1. Avoid using training views (+train) as they focus on known-view fitting. 2. Use test views for optimization. 3. Alternatively, use synthetic views sampled from the estimated camera trajectory (+syn.) [S4]. Typical number of synthetic views used: 50 [S4]. On the bicycle scene, PSNR with test views was Zoom-out 30.84, Zoom-in 24.23; with synthetic views: Zoom-out 29.72, Zoom-in 30.05 [S4].
+
+### Peripheral Model Initialization
+Creates a set of initial images from various views around the target volume and trains a peripheral model for 700 iterations using a MiniSplatting2-based approach [S5]. Renders training views at 512x512 pixels covering a 20° field of view [S5]. Number of initial views: 8, 12, 16, or 32. Samples per pixel (SPP): 4, 8, or 16. Normal quality preset: 12 views at 8 SPP. High quality preset: 16 views at 16 SPP [S5]. Using only 8 images or 4 SPP often misses important details. Using more than 16 views significantly delays the availability of the initial model with diminishing returns [S5]. Initial model generation takes under 300 ms; full reconstruction completes in about a second [S5]. Produces a higher-quality model faster than standard 3DGS with half the number of Gaussians, as opacity reset struggles to lower Gaussian counts [S5].
+
+### PUP (Pruning using Uncertainty/Second-order sensitivity)
+A pruning method that reduces the number of Gaussians ($N$) using a second-order sensitivity criterion based on the local reconstruction-loss change [S6]. Calculates the local Hessian block $H_j$ for each Gaussian's spatial parameters. Computes the sensitivity $\Delta L_j$. Removes Gaussians with low sensitivity (low $\Delta L_j$) before refinement [S6]. Formula: $\Delta L_j \approx \frac{1}{2} \delta\theta_j^T H_j \delta\theta_j$, where $\Delta L_j$ is the local reconstruction-loss change for Gaussian $j$, $\delta\theta_j$ is the perturbation of spatial Gaussian parameters $\theta_j$, and $H_j$ is the local Hessian block associated with the spatial parameters of Gaussian $j$ [S6]. On the shared baseline in Table 9, PUP removes approximately 90% of Gaussians and 90% of serialized bytes, changing PSNR from 25.01 to 25.42 dB [S6]. PUP primarily reduces $N$ (primitive count), whereas Compact3D primarily reduces $bg$ (storage per Gaussian) [S6].
 
